                <h2>Background: Why Insufficient Balance Becomes a Hidden Killer for Delivery</h2>
<p>In overseas ad delivery, running out of account balance causes delivery interruption that is often more damaging than creative decay. An unexpected pause can result in: campaign quality score decline, requiring an additional 3-5 days of recovery costs for cold restarts, and the bidding system treating your account as a low-quality player, causing CPM to increase by 15%-30%. A utility app operations team once reported that a balance depletion incident at midnight caused their daily acquisitions to drop to zero, directly losing over 200 potential paying users.</p>
<p>This article provides a directly actionable SOP to help you shift from reactive firefighting to proactive prevention.</p>
<h2>Step 1: Configure Multi-Level Balance Alert Mechanism</h2>
<h3>1.1 Determine Alert Thresholds</h3>
<p>Set three-level alert thresholds based on daily account spending and business tolerance:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Alert Level</th>
<th>Trigger Condition</th>
<th>Notification Method</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Initial Alert</td>
<td>Balance ≤ 3 days average daily spend</td>
<td>Email notification</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Medium Alert</td>
<td>Balance ≤ 1.5 days average daily spend</td>
<td>Email + SMS dual channel</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Urgent Alert</td>
<td>Balance ≤ 0.5 days average daily spend</td>
<td>Email + SMS + Phone call</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>1.2 Configure Alert Channels</h3>
<p>In Meta Business Manager, TikTok Ads Manager, or Google Ads, complete the following configuration:</p>
<ol>
<li>Log into ad account → Go to "Settings" → "Notification Preferences"</li>
<li>Enable balance-related notification options</li>
<li>Add at least two recipients (recommend including both campaign manager and finance contact)</li>
<li>Test whether alert notifications are successfully delivered</li>
</ol>
<h3>1.3 Integrate Third-Party Monitoring Tools</h3>
<p>For teams managing multiple accounts, it is recommended to use Yiyu Media's balance alert system:</p>
<ul>
<li>Supports batch import of multiple ad accounts</li>
<li>Customizable alert thresholds and notification frequency</li>
<li>Provides balance trend charts to assist with spend forecasting</li>
<li>Supports automatic campaign pause when balance falls below threshold</li>
</ul>
<h2>Step 2: Establish Standard Top-Up Procedures</h2>
<h3>2.1 Pre-Top-Up Preparation</h3>
<p>Upon receiving an alert notification, operations staff should complete the following confirmation within 2 hours:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Verify current balance and real-time spending today</li>
<li>[ ] Confirm current campaign budget and remaining delivery period</li>
<li>[ ] Check for pending invoices or historical arrears</li>
<li>[ ] Confirm available top-up quota with finance</li>
</ul>
<h3>2.2 Submit Top-Up Request</h3>
<p>Yiyu Media platform top-up operation steps:</p>
<ol>
<li>Log into Yiyu Media backend → Select target ad account</li>
<li>Click "Top Up" → Enter top-up amount (recommend covering at least 5-7 days of spending)</li>
<li>Confirm exchange rate and fees, then submit</li>
<li>Complete payment and upload payment receipt</li>
<li>Wait for balance confirmation (usually arrives within 1-4 hours)</li>
</ol>
<h3>2.3 Post-Top-Up Verification</h3>
<p>After top-up completion, be sure to confirm:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Refresh balance display in ad platform backend</li>
<li>[ ] Check if paused campaigns have automatically resumed</li>
<li>[ ] If not auto-resumed, manually enable and observe delivery status</li>
</ul>
<h2>Step 3: Multi-Account Balance Management Strategy</h2>
<p>When managing 5+ ad accounts, it is recommended to adopt the following strategies:</p>
<p><strong>Grouped Management</strong>: Divide accounts into high (&gt;$5000/day), medium ($1000-5000/day), and low (&lt;$1000/day) spend groups. Set more frequent check frequency for high-spend accounts.</p>
<p><strong>Central Pool Model</strong>: Maintain a certain reserve in the main account for allocation when balances fall below thresholds. This approach suits scenarios where budgets flow across campaigns in the short term.</p>
<p><strong>Smart Allocation Tools</strong>: Use Yiyu Media's multi-account management feature to set up cross-account balance alerts and automatic allocation rules, reducing manual monitoring pressure.</p>
<h2>Step 4: Delivery Interruption Emergency Response</h2>
<p>Even with alerts configured, unexpected situations can still occur. Here are the steps to follow after an interruption:</p>
<h3>4.2 First Response (0-30 Minutes)</h3>
<ol>
<li>Log into ad platform to confirm interruption cause</li>
<li>Immediately submit top-up or allocate balance</li>
<li>If already topped up, wait for balance to arrive</li>
</ol>
<h3>4.3 Resume Delivery (30 Minutes - 2 Hours)</h3>
<ol>
<li>Once balance arrives, prioritize resuming top 2-3 best-performing campaigns</li>
<li>Observe whether spend rate returns to normal</li>
<li>If needed, slightly increase bids to accelerate spend recovery</li>
</ol>
<h3>4.4 Follow-up Optimization (2-24 Hours)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Analyze spend loss during interruption period</li>
<li>Check whether any campaigns were downgraded by the system due to interruption</li>
<li>Adjust budget allocation to reduce future interruption risk</li>
</ul>
<h2>Key Checklist</h2>
<p>Before starting work each day, operations staff should confirm the following:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] All active account balances are sufficient, covering at least 48 hours of spending</li>
<li>[ ] Alert notification channels are functioning, no risk of missed alerts</li>
<li>[ ] Top-up process personnel (finance, account manager) are available</li>
<li>[ ] For cross-account allocation needs, pathways are planned in advance</li>
</ul>
<p>After establishing this SOP, teams can upgrade balance management from "firefighting response" to "preventive maintenance," effectively reducing delivery interruption risk and ensuring advertising delivery continuity and ROI stability.</p>
